# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles MUST keep LF endings.
# A CRLF shebang makes the Linux kernel report "no such file or directory" for the
# interpreter, which is a confusing way for a container to fail to start. Without this,
# a checkout on Windows with core.autocrlf=true would silently break the image.
*.sh                 text eol=lf
Dockerfile           text eol=lf
*.dockerfile         text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Keep these deterministic across platforms too.
*.yml            text eol=lf
*.yaml           text eol=lf
*.json           text eol=lf

# Every artefact of `scripts/brand/build_brand_package.py`. Its `--verify-no-drift` mode
# rebuilds the package and compares it to the committed copy BYTE FOR BYTE, because bytes
# are what catch a stale client artefact. So the canonical form of these files must not
# depend on the platform, or on a developer's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# The generator writes LF unconditionally; these keep the checkout agreeing with it.
# Without them a fresh Windows checkout reported all eleven package JSON files as drift
# while their content was identical -- the check describing the build machine rather than
# the brand package.
#
# `packages/brand/assets/**` is deliberately overridden back to -text further down.
packages/brand/**        text eol=lf
brand.generated.js       text eol=lf
tokens.generated.css     text eol=lf

# Incoming handoffs are IMMUTABLE EVIDENCE whose integrity is asserted by SHA-256
# manifests. Any line-ending normalisation rewrites their bytes, so a fresh clone would
# fail its own checksum verification and the evidence would no longer be evidence.
# "-text" disables all conversion and stores them exactly as delivered.
handoffs/incoming/**     -text

# Governance evidence (registrar letters, transcripts) must be byte-stable for the same
# reason: their checksums are recorded and re-verified.
evidence/governance/**   -text

# Generated asset copies under packages/brand/assets are byte-for-byte copies of the
# immutable Side A delivery and are verified against its SHA-256 digests. Git must not
# normalise their line endings: doing so rewrites the bytes, breaks the checksums and makes
# the brand-package drift check fail on any checkout with a different eol setting.
packages/brand/assets/** -text
